 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 233303,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<figure class=\"unnumbered\">\n<img src=\"exercises/media/d3ee7229faaee44c.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<figcaption>(credit: modification of work by Monique G. C. T. van Oijen and Pieter J. Slootweg/Clinical Cancer Research)</figcaption>\n</figure>",
   "stem_html": "<p>The graphs shows the tumor-suppressor gene, p53, and its mutations detected in cancer patients. The x-axis is the codon number of the gene. The y-axis is the frequency a mutation is detected in that codon.</p>\n<p>What is a conclusion we can draw from this graph?</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 723358,
     "content_html": "Mutations in the central region of the gene are more likely to cause cancer."
    },
    {
     "id": 723357,
     "content_html": "Mutations in the beggining parts of the gene are more likely to cause cancer."
    },
    {
     "id": 723356,
     "content_html": "The mutations are evenly distributed among the codons."
    },
    {
     "id": 723355,
     "content_html": "The mutations are concentrated in 5 codons. Mutations in other codons does not increase risk of cancer."
    }
   ],
